#+title: OpenOrg / Org2 launch architecture
#+subtitle: Product boundary, compatibility contract, and launch gates
#+date: 2026-08-22

* Decision state

=OpenOrg= is the proposed user-facing product name. =Org2= remains the open substrate.

The public rename is *provisional and blocked on name clearance*. A preliminary screen found an active company and product using =Open Org Workspace= for an AI-assisted document workspace. Owning =openorg.so= does not resolve that conflict. A review branch may prototype the name, onboarding, packaging, and site, but do not merge the public rename, point production DNS, distribute a release app, or announce the name until counsel reviews the overlap or a more distinctive name is selected.

* Proposed architecture

- *OpenOrg* :: The native workspace, product website, onboarding, integrations, and user experience.
- *Org2* :: The =.org2= format, compiler/runtime, CLI, npm package, schemas, repository, and editor tooling.
- *Product line* :: “OpenOrg is a local-first workspace for people and agents, built on the open Org2 format.”
- *Primary promise* :: “Run your work with people and agents from local files you control.”
- *Initial audience* :: Technical founders, operators, and knowledge workers who want agents working over files they own.

The boundary is architectural, not cosmetic: ordinary files remain canonical, the shared compiler owns semantics, and product clients consume that substrate without creating a private source of truth.

* Rename and compatibility matrix

| Surface | Proposed 0.5 presentation | Compatibility identifier | Initial action |
|---------+---------------------------+--------------------------+----------------|
| macOS Finder and menu-bar name | OpenOrg | =org.org2.workspace= | Prototype on the review branch; merge and distribute only after clearance. Preserve the bundle identifier. |
| macOS executable and Swift package | Internal implementation detail | =Org2Workspace= | Preserve for 0.5 to avoid packaging and diagnostics churn. |
| macOS preferences | Invisible implementation detail | =Org2Workspace.*= keys in =org.org2.workspace= | Preserve; do not migrate or reset existing settings. |
| macOS Keychain services | Invisible implementation detail | Existing =Org2Workspace.*= and =org.org2.workspace.*= service names | Preserve so tokens, source credentials, encryption settings, and pairings remain available. |
| macOS permissions | OpenOrg in user-facing permission copy | Existing TCC identity through =org.org2.workspace= | Preserve bundle identity; validate microphone, screen recording, speech, notifications, and accessibility after the display rename. |
| macOS release artifacts | OpenOrg Alpha | Historical =Org2Workspace= artifacts remain available | Publish =OpenOrg.dmg= and =OpenOrg-Intel.dmg= for =0.5.0+= without breaking historical download URLs. |
| iOS display name | OpenOrg | =org.org2.mobile= and existing app-group/push identifiers | Defer from primary onboarding; preserve identifiers and TestFlight continuity. |
| Corpus files | Org2 files | =.org2= and =.org= | Preserve permanently. |
| Corpus configuration | Org2 configuration | =org2.json= | Preserve permanently. |
| CLI and LSP | Org2 developer tooling | =org2= and =org2-lsp= | Preserve permanently. |
| npm package | Org2 developer package | =@aviaviavi/org2= | Preserve permanently. |
| Schemas and wire formats | Org2 contracts | Existing schema names and versions | Preserve; version only for semantic changes. |
| GitHub repository | Org2 source repository | =aviaviavi/org2= | Preserve for launch. |
| VS Code and editor extensions | Org2 developer tooling | Existing extension identifiers | Preserve; explain that OpenOrg is built on Org2. |
| Product website | OpenOrg | Proposed =openorg.so= | Hold DNS and public launch until name clearance. |
| Developer documentation | Org2 developer documentation | Existing technical URLs may redirect later | Keep accessible and clearly subordinate to the product story. |

* Compatibility invariants

The first product-name release must prove all of the following before shipping:

1. Installing the renamed app over the current app retains the selected corpus and mounted corpora.
2. Preferences, AI destinations, transcription settings, and appearance settings remain intact.
3. Existing Keychain credentials remain readable without re-entry.
4. Existing TCC permissions continue to apply or the app explains exactly which permission must be renewed.
5. Global capture, meeting recording, mobile pairing, push notifications, and deep links still reach the same app.
6. Existing =.org2= files, =org2.json= configuration, runs, workflows, agent profiles, and goals require no migration.
7. The =org2= CLI, npm package, schemas, LSP, and editor integrations remain backwards compatible.
8. Historical release and download URLs remain available.

These invariants are partially guarded by =npm run check:openorg-compat=. UI migration and TCC behavior still require installed-app tests on a signed candidate.

* Launch shape after clearance

- Release name :: =OpenOrg Alpha=
- Target version :: =0.5.0=
- Product site :: =openorg.so=
- Developer substrate :: Org2, linked from an “OpenOrg is built on Org2” architecture page
- Primary demonstration :: Open files → inspect Agenda → delegate bounded work → review citations and durable output → approve or revise → open the same =.org2= file elsewhere

Keep iOS, data notebooks, publishing, encryption, and source connectors available but outside the five-minute first-run story.

* Gates

** Name gate

- Obtain a professional clearance opinion covering exact and confusingly similar marks in the relevant software, SaaS, productivity, and AI-assistant classes and common-law use.
- Resolve the existing =Open Org Workspace= overlap through counsel, coexistence/consent, or a different product name.
- Only then point =openorg.so= at the product site or rename distributed binaries.

** Product gates

- Notarized Mac build that installs normally.
- Existing users retain settings, permissions, credentials, and corpus access.
- Fresh users reach the cited review loop within ten minutes.
- At least five outside users complete the core flow and at least three return within seven days.
- Release, docs, macOS, corpus, and lifecycle checks pass.
- Backup, data-sharing, agent-action, and current-alpha boundaries are documented where users install and start the product.

* Preliminary name screen

This is a pragmatic knockout screen, not legal advice or a comprehensive clearance search.

- Checked 2026-08-22: the exact concatenated =OpenOrg= and exact spaced =Open Org= searches returned no exact record in the USPTO federal trademark search interface.
- [[https://www.uspto.gov/trademarks/search/federal-trademark-searching][USPTO guidance]] says a federal database lookup is only one step and that confusing similarity and related goods/services matter.
- [[https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/15030370][Open Org Group Ltd]] is an active UK company incorporated in 2023.
- [[https://www.openorg.fyi/][Open Org]] currently markets an =Open Org Workspace= with AI document creation, search, saved chat, files, and “second brain” positioning. That is close enough in name and commercial impression to be a material launch risk even without an exact U.S. registration.
- The UK IPO register blocked automated access during this screen, and WIPO's official Global Brand Database could not be reached from the test environment. Those searches, state registrations, common-law use, app stores, domains, and phonetic/similar marks remain outstanding.

* Immediate safe work

While the name gate is unresolved, continue work that is useful under any final product name:

1. Preserve and automatically check compatibility identifiers.
2. Design the five-minute first-run flow without branding it.
3. Complete signing and notarization automation.
4. Document data handling, backups, agent actions, and current alpha limitations in the installation and onboarding flow.
5. Prepare a product-site information architecture and demo script using a replaceable brand token.
